
This directory is intended for PlatformIO Test Runner and project tests.

Unit Testing is a software testing method by which individual units of
source code, sets of one or more MCU program modules together with associated
control data, usage procedures, and operating procedures, are tested to
determine whether they are fit for use. Unit testing finds problems early
in the development cycle.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing:
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html

MQTT delivery and recovery regression tests run on the host without board
libraries or a broker:

clang++ -std=c++11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -fsanitize=address,undefined -Iinclude test/test_mqtt_delivery/test_main.cpp -o test/az3166-test-mqtt
./test/az3166-test-mqtt
rm test/az3166-test-mqtt

These tests use a nonblocking fake socket to cover fragmented CONNACK/PUBACK,
rejected or incomplete acknowledgements, missing replies, disconnects, partial
writes, packet size limits, consecutive delivery failures and timer rollover.

OTA storage/parser/vector/legacy-configuration tests:

clang++ -std=c++11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -fsanitize=address,undefined -Iinclude test/test_ota/test_main.cpp -o test/az3166-test-ota
./test/az3166-test-ota
rm test/az3166-test-ota
node test/test_ota/test_checksum.js

The production shared recovery engine has a separate sanitizer-enabled NOR
model. It uses real region/sector sizes, real SHA-256, commit-last records,
and reset injection before/within/after every modeled persistent operation.
It does not exercise a physical flash controller or prove brownout behavior:

python3 test/test_ota/run_recovery.py

Cases include three persisted attempts, no automatic retry, interrupted
installation/restoration/promotion and first provisioning, corruption,
incompatible image/config/watchdog ABI, full blank-ownership checks, ambiguous
generations, rollover, read/write failures, committed-confirmation readback
failure, staging reuse and the exact 608 KiB image limit. Local-health checks
include minimum loop progress and millisecond rollover.

Build both aliases with the supported GCC 6.3.1 toolchain:
pio run -e az3166_app -e mxchip_az3166

Build the experimental recovery application and source-only boot component
without programming anything (supported Linux/amd64 GCC 6.3.1 environment):

pio run -e az3166_recovery
python3 bootloader/build.py --core-dir /root/.platformio

The source build validates boot ABI/vectors/size and excludes SDK/RTOS,
allocation and option-byte programming entry points. Controlled one-board
tests passed three actual watchdog failures and interrupted install/restore/
promotion, using private fixtures and temporary hardware breakpoints rather
than shipping fault hooks. This does not qualify brownouts or other devices.
RAM-only unhealthy local health also exhausted three hardware-watchdog trials.
WiFi/DNS outages and the existing MQTT-delivery reboot policy did not cause
rollback. Final full-flash readback verified protected boot, production image,
fallback, metadata and unchanged configuration/authentication/RF bytes.
Separate bootstrap/protection approval and private backups remain mandatory;
see bootloader/README.md. The browser hardware harness below is for the existing
vendor OTA path, not a substitute for controlled recovery testing.

The opt-in hardware browser test performs a REAL firmware installation.
Read OTA.md, retain ST-Link recovery, install pyserial and Playwright/Chromium,
and keep power connected. Run --help first:

python test/test_ota/hardware_browser.py --help

Supply a private mode-0600 credentials JSON containing url, username and
password; do not put credentials in command arguments or git. Example invocation:

python test/test_ota/hardware_browser.py --credentials-file /private/operator.json --image /private/firmware.bin --expected-version 1.1.1 --serial /dev/cu.usbmodem12303 --log /private/new-ota-test.log --install

The log must not already exist. It is created mode 0600 and can contain private
device settings from the serial console. The test uses actual browser HTTP
hashing/upload, rejects wrong-hash and stale apply, verifies reset is blocked
during staging, installs, compares settings, reauthenticates, and waits for two
MQTT PUBACKs after boot. It never interrupts bootloader installation.
